1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,480
Hi, I'm Dave from the Burps Channel Development team and today I'm going to talk a little bit

2
00:00:06,480 --> 00:00:12,480
about the global crawl path view that is coming in Burp Suite 2023.8. So in previous versions of

3
00:00:12,480 --> 00:00:18,080
Burp Suite, a common workflow that people had was to contribute information to the target tab by

4
00:00:18,080 --> 00:00:22,720
using other tools in Burp. So you could use the proxy or you could run a crawl task. But once

5
00:00:22,720 --> 00:00:26,880
you'd run a crawl task, all the information about the path that the crawl took around the application

6
00:00:26,880 --> 00:00:33,360
were lost and they were only available to that crawl task itself. Coming in Burp Suite 2023.8,

7
00:00:33,920 --> 00:00:38,400
you have the ability to run multiple crawls of the same target and have all of your crawl tasks

8
00:00:38,400 --> 00:00:43,200
contribute to the global view of that target. So let's jump into Burp Suite and see how it works.

9
00:00:44,160 --> 00:00:49,760
So here in Burp Suite 2023.8, I've got a crawl task of Gin and Juice Shop, which is Portswigger's

10
00:00:49,760 --> 00:00:55,440
deliberately vulnerable web application. And in the target tab, I can see all of the paths that the

11
00:00:55,440 --> 00:01:01,360
crawler took when I first crawled the application. However, that crawl was restricted to only allow

12
00:01:01,360 --> 00:01:06,560
it to go one link deep in the application. So if I start a second crawl of the application that's

13
00:01:06,560 --> 00:01:13,840
allowing it to do more actions, we'll be able to see that the crawl task will start contributing and

14
00:01:13,840 --> 00:01:19,520
it will start populating the information from the previous crawl task. So even though it's an entirely

15
00:01:19,520 --> 00:01:24,400
separate crawl task, it still remembers that the action it took this time matches the same one that

16
00:01:24,400 --> 00:01:29,440
it saw that was possible last time. And that means that you can actually run multiple tasks with

17
00:01:29,440 --> 00:01:35,760
different settings and have them all contribute to the same view of the application. This has really

18
00:01:35,760 --> 00:01:42,080
powerful implications because if you can create a total view of what is possible to do on the site by

19
00:01:42,080 --> 00:01:46,960
crawling it and exploring different interesting parts of the content, you can really map out an

20
00:01:46,960 --> 00:01:54,400
application to its absolute fullest. In some cases, you might want to run a task and not have it

21
00:01:54,400 --> 00:01:58,800
contribute to the global view though. For example, if you were playing around with different settings

22
00:01:58,800 --> 00:02:03,280
and trying to get the best settings for your crawl, you might want to run a crawl in complete isolation.

23
00:02:04,160 --> 00:02:10,160
So to do that, you go to the Scan Launcher and you tick the new Run Isolated Scan button.

24
00:02:10,160 --> 00:02:17,280
If you run an isolated scan, you'll see that the Target tab appears for that scan as well as the

25
00:02:17,280 --> 00:02:22,080
Target tab in the rest of Burp. And actually, these two things are completely isolated and they won't

26
00:02:22,080 --> 00:02:26,160
contribute to each other and they won't conflict with each other. You still have the Crawl Past tab

27
00:02:26,160 --> 00:02:30,560
as was available in previous versions of Burp as well as the Sitemap for that task specifically.

28
00:02:31,520 --> 00:02:35,440
We're still thinking of ways in which we can improve this functionality and help you get even more out of

29
00:02:35,440 --> 00:02:49,840
scans. So for now, give it a try and let us know any feedback that you have.

